WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.720
Whenever we take a photo, we capture a&nbsp;
piece of reality from one viewpoint. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:17.040
if we have multiple cameras on our smartphone, a&nbsp;
few viewpoints at most. In an earlier episode, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.960
explored how to upgrade these to 3D photos, where&nbsp;
we could we could kind of look behind the person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:30.000
I am saying kind of, because what we see&nbsp;
here is not reality, this is statistical data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.680
that is filled in by an algorithm to match&nbsp;
its surroundings, which we refer to as image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:43.760
inpainting. So strictly speaking, it is likely&nbsp;
information, but not necessarily true information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.840
and also, we can recognize the synthetic parts&nbsp;
of the image as they are significantly blurrier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.080
So the question naturally arises in the mind&nbsp;
of the curious Scholar, how about actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.680
looking behind the person. Is that somehow&nbsp;
possible or is that still science fiction?

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.600
Well, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
because this technique shows us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:12.240
the images of the future by sticking a&nbsp;
bunch of cameras onto a spherical shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:20.160
and when we capture a video, it will see…something&nbsp;
like this. And the goal is to untangle this mess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.280
and we’re not done yet, we also need to&nbsp;
reconstruct the geometry of the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.320
as if the video was captured from many different&nbsp;
viewpoints at the same time. Absolutely amazing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.080
And yes, this means that we can change&nbsp;
our viewpoint while the video is running.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.080
Since it is doing the reconstruction in layers,&nbsp;
we know how far each object is in these scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:52.480
enabling us to rotate these sparks and&nbsp;
flames and look at them in 3D. Yum.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.160
Now, I am a light transport researcher by&nbsp;
trade, so I hope you can tell that I am very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.440
happy about these beautiful volumetric&nbsp;
effects, but I would also love to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:10.160
how it deals with reflective surfaces. Let’s see&nbsp;
together…look at the reflections in the sand here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:17.200
and I’ll add a lot of camera movement and&nbsp;
wow…this thing works. It really works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.840
and it does not break a sweat even if&nbsp;
we try a more reflective surface…or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.560
an even more reflective surface! This&nbsp;
is as reflective as it gets I’m afraid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.840
and we still get a consistent and&nbsp;
crisp image in the mirror. Bravo!

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.120
Alright, let’s get a little more&nbsp;
greedy, what about seeing through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:46.960
thin fences…that is quite a challenge.&nbsp;
And…look at the tail wags there. This is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.720
a touch blurrier here and there,&nbsp;
but overall, very impressive.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.960
So what do we do with a video like this?&nbsp;
Well, we can use our mouse to look around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.360
within the photo in our web browser,&nbsp;
you can try this yourself right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.960
by clicking on the paper in the video description.&nbsp;
Make sure to follow the instructions if you do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:12.800
Or we can make the viewing experience even more&nbsp;
immersive with a head-mounted display, where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.640
of course, the image will follow wherever&nbsp;
we turn our head. Both of these truly feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.440
like entering a photograph and getting&nbsp;
a feel of the room therein. Loving it.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.280
Now, since there is a lot of information in these&nbsp;
Light Field Videos, it also needs a powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.080
internet connection to relay them. Even when using&nbsp;
H.265, a powerful video compression standard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.320
we are talking in the order of hundreds of&nbsp;
megabits. It is like streaming several videos in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:49.200
4k resolution at the same time. Compression helps,&nbsp;
however, we also have to make sure that we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.360
compress too much, so that compression artifacts&nbsp;
don’t eat the content behind thin geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.120
or at least, not too much. I bet this will&nbsp;
be an interesting topic for a followup paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.520
so make sure to subscribe and hit the&nbsp;
bell icon to not miss it when it appears.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:12.160
And for now, more practical light field photos and&nbsp;
videos will be available that allow us to almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.360
feel like we are really in the room with the&nbsp;
subjects of the videos. What a time to be alive!

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.080
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

